    {
      "type": "grammar",
      "heading": "A first look at Punjabi grammar",
      "body": "Punjabi follows a subject–object–verb (SOV) word order: the verb comes last. English says \"you HOW are\" order as \"how are you\", but Punjabi builds the same question as \"tusi (you) kiven (how) ho (are)\".\n\nPunjabi also has two words for \"you\": ਤੁਸੀਂ (tusi) is polite — use it with people you don't know well and with elders. ਤੂੰ (tu) is informal, used with close friends and children. When in doubt, always choose tusi."
    },
    {
      "type": "whyThisWay",
      "heading": "Why is it said this way?",
      "body": "\"Sat sri akal\" is more than a hello — it is a short religious greeting meaning roughly \"God is the ultimate truth\", shared across Sikh and wider Punjabi communities. That is why it works in almost every situation: formal, informal, meeting or parting.\n\nAnd \"tusi kiven ho\" puts the verb last because Punjabi sentences are built verb-final. Once you get used to verbs living at the end, Punjabi word order starts to feel natural instead of backwards."
    },
    {
      "type": "conversation",
      "heading": "A short conversation",
      "conversation": {
        "title": "Meeting a neighbour",
        "context": "Amrit greets her neighbour Baldev in the morning.",
        "lines": [
          {
            "speaker": "Amrit",
            "line": {
              "gurmukhi": "ਸਤ ਸ੍ਰੀ ਅਕਾਲ ਜੀ!",
              "romanised": "Sat sri akal ji!",
              "english": "Hello! (adding \"ji\" makes it warmer/more respectful)"
            }
          },
          {
            "speaker": "Baldev",
            "line": {
              "gurmukhi": "ਸਤ ਸ੍ਰੀ ਅਕਾਲ! ਤੁਸੀਂ ਕਿਵੇਂ ਹੋ?",
              "romanised": "Sat sri akal! Tusi kiven ho?",
              "english": "Hello! How are you?"
            }
          },
          {
            "speaker": "Amrit",
            "line": {
              "gurmukhi": "ਮੈਂ ਠੀਕ ਹਾਂ, ਧੰਨਵਾਦ। ਤੁਸੀਂ?",
              "romanised": "Main theek haan, dhannvaad. Tusi?",
              "english": "I am fine, thank you. And you?"
            }
          },
          {
            "speaker": "Baldev",
            "line": {
              "gurmukhi": "ਮੈਂ ਵੀ ਠੀਕ ਹਾਂ।",
              "romanised": "Main vi theek haan.",
              "english": "I am fine too. (vi = also/too)"
            }
          }
        ]
      }
    },
